---
import SiteShell from "../layouts/SiteShell.astro";
import { loadSite } from "../lib/site";

const site = loadSite();
const house = site.house.find((item) => item.path === "/how-this-works");
if (!house) {
  throw new Error("house page missing from catalog");
}
---

<SiteShell
  page="house"
  path={house.path}
  title={house.title}
  description={house.description}
  vintageId={site.vintage_id}
>
  <h1>{house.h1}</h1>
  <p>
    Prism gathers official government statistics into one portrait of India: what
    the state collects, spends, and delivers, and how people live. High or low,
    good or bad — the reader decides.
  </p>

  <h2>What you are reading</h2>
  <p>
    Each topic page starts with a citizen question. It answers with the published
    fact, then the evidence, then how that fact was measured. The wording is
    written once. The numbers are filled from a snapshot of official
    observations, citations, and caveats. Opening the page does not fetch a
    ministry website.
  </p>

  <h2>How a number gets here</h2>
  <p>For every figure on a page:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The page names the question it is answering.</li>
    <li>It names the producing office.</li>
    <li>
      It uses a file from an official government agency that can source that
      table — the producing office first, another government host if that is
      where the file lives.
    </li>
    <li>Producer, series, date, and fetch source sit with the number.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    News write-ups, private polls, and international secondary databases are not
    the source of record.
  </p>

  <h2>What this is not</h2>
  <p>
    Prism is not a newsroom, a think tank, or an advocacy shop. It does not
    forecast. It does not score ministries or parties. It does not replace the
    agencies that collect the data.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where a page ranks units, it is ranking a published measure this snapshot can
    bind — not a government scorecard. Named analysis of official series may
    appear as "Analysis by Prism" beside the producer cite. The agency remains
    the producer.
  </p>

  <h2>When the record is incomplete</h2>
  <p>
    If a series breaks, lags, or two official numbers disagree, the page says so
    in the same view as the number. Places the series does not cover are named.
    What is <em>not published</em> stays empty — never filled with a remembered
    figure.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/sources">Sources</a> points to the pages in this snapshot, where
    each number names its producing office.
  </p>
</SiteShell>
